---
import Base from "@layouts/Base.astro";
import {siteConfig} from "~/settings";
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';

const features = [
    {
        title: "Many packs, one repository",
        body: "Host every mod pack your community uses in one place. Packs can depend on each other, so a base pack with core mods can be extended by event-specific packs.",
    },
    {
        title: "Delta patching",
        body: "Only the parts of a PBO that changed between versions are downloaded. Saves bandwidth for both server and players.",
    },
    {
        title: "Speedy",
        body: "pamm is written to be fast, be it downloading changes or building the repo server side.",
    },
    {
        title: "Packs are cheap",
        body: "A pack is just a couple of files. Easy to create and delete, which makes one-off event packs easy.",
    },
    {
        title: "Optional addons",
        body: "Packs can ship optional addons that players toggle on or off.",
    },
    {
        title: "External addons",
        body: "Players can load local addons, with their workshop subscriptions being auto detected.",
    },
    {
        title: "Local packs",
        body: "Packs that only exist on your machine, for testing, mission making or private mod lists.",
    },
    {
        title: "Server deployment (experimental)",
        body: "Deploy a pack to a dedicated server. pamm will symlink/copy the addons to the right place, copy their keys to the right directory, write the launch params and fire up the server.",
    },
    {
        title: "Mission maker mode",
        body: "Extra tools for mission makers in the GUI: launching without optionals, manage local packs, jumping straight to the repo on disk",
    },
    {
        title: "Full Linux support",
        body: "Runs natively on Linux, with native, Flatpak and snap Steam fully supported.",
    },
    {
        title: "GUI and CLI",
        body: "All player features are available in both, server administration tools only via cli",
    },
    {
        title: "Customizable GUI",
        body: "Chang the color of the GUI to fit the style of your unit, add your own icons and banner images",
    },
];
---

<Base>
    <section class="hero">
        <Image src="/logo.png" alt="The pamm logo" width="160" height="160" loading="eager"/>
        <hgroup>
            <h1>pamm</h1>
            <p>Personal ARMA Mod Manager</p>
        </hgroup>
        <p>
            A lightweight, fast Arma 3 mod manager for communities that host their own
            mods.
        </p>
        <p class="actions">
            <a
                    href={`${siteConfig.repoUrl}/releases/latest`}
                    role="button"
                    target="_blank"
                    rel="noopener noreferrer">Download</a
            >
            <a
                    href={siteConfig.repoUrl}
                    role="button"
                    class="secondary outline"
                    target="_blank"
                    rel="noopener noreferrer">View on GitHub</a
            >
        </p>
    </section>

    <section id="features">
        <h2>Features</h2>
        <div class="features">
            {
                features.map((f) => (
                        <article>
                            <h3>{f.title}</h3>
                            <p>{f.body}</p>
                        </article>
                ))
            }
        </div>
    </section>

    <section id="packs">
        <h2>Repositories and packs</h2>
        <p>
            A <strong>repository</strong> is what your community hosts: all behind just one URL.
            It holds any number of <strong>packs</strong>. A pack is a collection of addons that
            players sync and launch, with its own description, icon and launch
            parameters.
        </p>
        <p>
            Each pack can have one <strong>parent</strong>. A pack inherits all of its
            parent's addons, and the parent inherits from its own parent, so you can
            build chains as long as you need:
        </p>
        <pre><code>My Community              repository
├── core                  CBA, ACE, TFAR, ...
│   ├── campaign          core + campaign terrains and assets
│   │   └── campaign-ww2  core + campaign + WW2 mods
│   └── training          core + training maps
└── modern-event          standalone pack, no parent</code></pre>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>Sync</strong> downloads the whole chain. Addons are not replicated, so they're downloaded just
                once no matter how many child packs depend on it.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Launch</strong> loads the pack's own addons plus the required addons
                of every ancestor. Launch parameters are per pack.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Optional addons</strong> from every pack in the chain show up when
                you launch a child pack. Your choice is saved on the pack that owns the addon,
                so enabling an optional in <code>core</code> enables it for every pack built
                on <code>core</code>.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Deploy</strong> resolves the same chain on the server, so a dedicated
                server loads the same required addons as players. Optional addons are left
                out.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Local packs</strong> can use any pack as their parent, including a
                pack from the server or another local pack. These are your local playground for mission making, mod
                developing or testing.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            On disk, every pack is its own folder.
            Adding packs can be easily done via the CLI and once built will become available to all players.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section id="get-started">
        <h2>Get started</h2>
        <div class="grid">
            <article>
                <header><strong>For players</strong></header>
                <p>
                    Install the desktop app or cli from the <a
                        href={`${siteConfig.repoUrl}/releases/latest`}
                        target="_blank"
                        rel="noopener noreferrer">latest release</a
                > and add your community's repository URL:
                </p>
                <pre><code>pamm_cli init-remote &lt;url&gt;  # join a repo
pamm_cli sync &lt;pack&gt;          # download changes
pamm_cli launch &lt;pack&gt;        # start Arma</code></pre>
            </article>
            <article>
                <header><strong>For server admins</strong></header>
                <p>
                    Init a new repo using the CLI, add packs and drop your addons into the right folders. Then build the
                    repo to populate the <code>www/</code> folder.
                </p>
                <pre><code>pamm_cli init       # create an empty repo
pamm_cli add-pack   # register a pack
# drop addons into &lt;pack&gt;/addons/
pamm_cli build      # populate www/</code></pre>
                <p>
                    <small
                    >Point your web server at <code>&lt;repo&gt;/www/</code>, share the URL, and you are done
                    </small>
                </p>
            </article>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section id="linux">
        <h2>Linux Support</h2>
        <p>
            On Linux, pamm launches the Windows version of Arma 3 through Steam and
            Proton. Arma can live in any of your Steam libraries.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>Guided Steam setup.</strong> Run <code>pamm_cli linux</code> or click <em
            >Steam setup</em
            > in the GUI to see exactly which launch options or Flatpak
                overrides your mod location needs.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Proton tools.</strong> Open a file browser inside the prefix, or stop
                everything running in it, including the game.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>TeamSpeak in the Proton prefix.</strong> Install and launch the Windows
                TeamSpeak 3 client in the same prefix as the game.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Follows your Proton version.</strong> Switch Arma to another Proton
                version in Steam and pamm picks it up.
            </li>
        </ul>
    </section>
</Base>

<style>
    .hero {
        text-align: center;
        padding-block: var(--pico-block-spacing-vertical);
    }

    .actions {
        display: flex;
        flex-wrap: wrap;
        justify-content: center;
        gap: var(--pico-spacing);
    }

    .hero hgroup {
        margin-block: 1rem;
    }

    .features {
        display: grid;
        grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(16rem, 1fr));
        gap: var(--pico-grid-column-gap);
    }

    .features article {
        margin: 0;
    }

    .features h3 {
        font-size: 1.1rem;
    }

    .features p {
        margin: 0;
    }

    section {
        scroll-margin-top: 1rem;
    }

    #features > p {
        margin-top: var(--pico-spacing);
    }
</style>
